    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d191">

                <pb facs="0315" n="239" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg239"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.4061 Motion Pictures/223: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Marriner</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-04T18:00:00-05:00">October 4, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>396. Your despatch 2162.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d191-f1"/> You are requested to
                    make formal inquiry of the French Government concerning the nature of the
                    proposed <pb facs="0316" n="240" xml:id="pg240"/> film decree on the grounds
                    that press reports and rumors indicate that American film interests may be
                    materially injured by its provisions.</p>
                <p>For your information, in connection with the trade agreement we have requested
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) permits to show 100 dubbed films; (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) permits to show original version films in 10
                    theaters in Paris and 20 theaters in other departments; (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >c</hi>) quota of 93,900 kilograms. The proposed decree would have the
                    effect of making any valuable concession on films impossible, thus jeopardizing
                    the whole trade agreement.</p>
                <p>You may use your discretion as to whether to make representations to the French
                    authorities verbally or in writing but you are requested to impress
                    energetically upon them the lamentable effect the promulgation of such a decree
                    during trade negotiations would have upon American opinion.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
